 {"status": "complete", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-23", "title": "[P2] The db pack blocks the remediation and permits the disease: `sed`/`git commit`/read-only SELECT of the phrase block, while `psql -f <file>` that really drops the table is allowed", "priority": "P2", "cell": "interruptor rule pack db", "detail": "Pattern rules match the statement shape anywhere in the command string (documented as deliberate), which in a DB pack inverts the cost/benefit. Measured through the bridge with the pack live: `psql -c \"DROP TABLE users\"` -> block pack-db-drop-table (good); `psql -f migrations/002_legacy.sql` (the file DOES drop a table) -> allow/null (pre-existing DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-10 file-body gap); `sed -i \"s/DROP DATABASE/-- DROP DATABASE/\" migrations/003_shard.sql` -> block pack-db-drop-database; `git commit -am \"park the DROP DATABASE migration\"` -> block pack-db-drop-database; `psql -c \"SELECT msg FROM t WHERE msg = 'drop database retry'\"` (read-only) -> block; `echo \"DROP TABLE users\" > build/rollback.sql` -> allow (allow-echo). Net: the user is stopped from editing, committing or querying the phrase, and not stopped from executing it from a file. Fix direction: constrain the pack's two block rules to execution context rather than bare text (e.g. require the SQL client + a non-select statement, or exclude quoted string literals, `sed` replacement expressions and git -m messages), and pair the pack with the file-body fix so the destructive path it targets is actually covered. Regression cases: the six shapes above.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, 15-command DB work session + 8-shape matrix", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": "2", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/terminal-jail-DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-23", "branch": "wt/DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-23", "updated_at": "2026-09-20 10:52:42", "worker_status": "failed", "commit_hash": "7573df5d4a0b7a8f261f7508bc3